## 3. System Architecture

Dayflow is a **modular monolith**: a single Next.js deployment that renders the UI, exposes an
API, enforces auth, and talks to MySQL through Prisma. Reads happen in **Server Components**;
mutations go through **Route Handlers** (`/api/*`) and/or **Server Actions**. A single
`middleware.ts` guards routes and enforces roles at the edge.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    subgraph Client["Browser"]
        UI["React Server + Client Components<br/>Tailwind + shadcn/ui"]
    end

    subgraph Next["Next.js App (App Router) — single deployment"]
        MW["middleware.ts<br/>Auth + RBAC route guard"]
        RSC["Server Components<br/>(data reads)"]
        RH["Route Handlers /api/*<br/>(REST endpoints)"]
        SA["Server Actions<br/>(form mutations)"]
        AUTH["Auth.js (NextAuth v5)<br/>Credentials + JWT session"]
    end

    subgraph DataLayer["Data Layer"]
        PRISMA["Prisma Client (singleton)"]
        DB[("MySQL 8")]
    end

    UI -->|navigate / fetch| MW
    UI -->|form submit| SA
    MW --> RSC
    MW --> RH
    RSC --> PRISMA
    RH --> AUTH
    RH --> PRISMA
    SA --> AUTH
    SA --> PRISMA
    AUTH --> PRISMA
    PRISMA --> DB
```

### Layered responsibilities

- **Presentation** — App Router pages, layouts, and components. Role-aware navigation.
- **Application / API** — Route Handlers + Server Actions. Validate input (Zod), enforce RBAC, call the domain layer.
- **Domain / Services** — plain functions in `src/lib/services/*` encapsulating business rules (e.g. compute `workHours`, prevent overlapping leave, block double check-in).
- **Data access** — Prisma Client singleton (`src/lib/prisma.ts`).
- **Persistence** — MySQL, schema owned by Prisma migrations.

